       intervals: size = 119
       intervals [1]:
           xmin = 1.71
           xmax = 8.69
           text = "And welcome back to Pronunciation with Emma. In today's video I'm going to give you a complete"
       intervals [2]:
           xmin = 8.69
           xmax = 13.89
           text = "masterclass on sentence stress and intonation in English. Make sure you stay until the end of the"
       intervals [3]:
           xmin = 13.89
           xmax = 18.29
           text = "video because I have a really nice surprise for you that will help you practice sentence stress"
       intervals [4]:
           xmin = 18.29
           xmax = 24.45
           text = "and intonation more and get feedback on it. So let's start firstly with what kind of stress"
       intervals [5]:
           xmin = 24.45
           xmax = 30.53
           text = "English uses. English is a stress time language and this basically means that you give certain"
       intervals [6]:
           xmin = 30.53
           xmax = 36.93
           text = "words, which we'll talk about in a moment, more stress and more emphasis when other words are"
       intervals [7]:
           xmin = 36.93
           xmax = 42.05
           text = "weakened. They sound a lot softer. This is why when you're watching something with subtitles,"
       intervals [8]:
           xmin = 42.05
           xmax = 47.49
           text = "for example, you understand everything and then you turn off the subtitles and you think,"
       intervals [9]:
           xmin = 47.49
           xmax = 52.11
           text = "what? What was that word? That sounded like a really long word. But actually when you put the"
       intervals [10]:
           xmin = 52.11
           xmax = 58.03
           text = "subtitles back on and you take a look, it actually turns out to be these little words all squished"
       intervals [11]:
           xmin = 58.03
           xmax = 64.03
           text = "together and said very quickly and that's because of sentence stress. We can compare a stress time"
       intervals [12]:
           xmin = 64.03
           xmax = 69.79
           text = "language to, for example, a syllable time language. A syllable time language basically gives each"
       intervals [13]:
           xmin = 69.79
           xmax = 75.47
           text = "syllable approximately the same amount of time. We can also compare it to mora time languages,"
       intervals [14]:
           xmin = 75.47
           xmax = 81.07
           text = "for example, Japanese. So if you're a Japanese speaker, congrats, you speak a mora time language."
       intervals [15]:
           xmin = 81.07
           xmax = 87.23
           text = "This is very similar in essence to syllable time languages where you give each mora the same amount"
       intervals [16]:
           xmin = 87.23
           xmax = 92.99
           text = "of time. I still remember when I was at university, a lecturer of mine described mora timing and"
       intervals [17]:
           xmin = 92.99
           xmax = 99.07
           text = "syllable time languages like machine guns, it's like da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da. While English is more like"
       intervals [18]:
           xmin = 99.07
           xmax = 106.27
           text = "ba-ba-ba-ba-ba, ba-ba-ba, ba-ba-ba-ba-ba. And that's because we give certain words more stress"
       intervals [19]:
           xmin = 106.27
           xmax = 111.63
           text = "in a language like English. So let me give you an example of what I mean. Let's take this exercise"
       intervals [20]:
           xmin = 111.63
           xmax = 116.43
           text = "here. Very simple one, okay. I hope you can count to four because that's all you need to do right now."
       intervals [21]:
           xmin = 117.23
           xmax = 126.46
           text = "So we've got 1, 2, 3, 4. Okay? That's the rhythm we're going to follow. 1, 2, 3, 4."
       intervals [22]:
           xmin = 126.46
           xmax = 135.32
           text = "Let's try adding some words in between. For example, 1 and 2 and 3 and 4. Now notice what"
       intervals [23]:
           xmin = 135.32
           xmax = 145.97
           text = "I did. I didn't say 1 and 2 and 3 and 4. I didn't stress and. I only stressed the numbers."
       intervals [24]:
           xmin = 145.97
           xmax = 156.06
           text = "And was weakened to and. So not and, but and. We're using a weak form here. 1 and 2 and"
       intervals [25]:
           xmin = 156.06
           xmax = 166.23
           text = "3 and 4. Let's add another word in there. 1 and a 2 and a 3 and a 4. 1 and a 2 and a"
       intervals [26]:
           xmin = 166.23
           xmax = 174.65
           text = "3 and a 4. Again, I'm not saying 1 and a 2 and a 3 and a 4. Okay? That's quite tiring"
       intervals [27]:
           xmin = 174.65
           xmax = 181.25
           text = "and my poor hand is gonna start hurting in a moment. But you see what I mean. So in English"
       intervals [28]:
           xmin = 181.25
           xmax = 186.41
           text = "we don't stress every single word. We're going to stress the numbers because they're important"
       intervals [29]:
           xmin = 186.41
           xmax = 192.81
           text = "here in this exercise. But the and and a are going to be weakened to and a. We're going"
       intervals [30]:
           xmin = 192.81
           xmax = 200.37
           text = "to join them together. And a. And a. 1 and a 2 and a 3 and a 4. Now let's apply this"
       intervals [31]:
           xmin = 200.37
           xmax = 209.49
           text = "same rule to a proper sentence. So here I've got, I want a cup of tea. I want a cup of"
       intervals [32]:
           xmin = 209.49
           xmax = 217.29
           text = "tea. So notice I'm not saying, I want a cup of tea. It's not all stress. I want a cup"
       intervals [33]:
           xmin = 217.29
           xmax = 222.65
           text = "of tea. Now when it comes to intonation, we're going to raise our pitch slightly on those"
       intervals [34]:
           xmin = 222.65
           xmax = 227.89
           text = "stressed words. So we're not going to read it all monotone like, I want a cup of tea."
       intervals [35]:
           xmin = 227.89
           xmax = 233.49
           text = "Okay? It sounds very robotic in English if you do that. You'll be understood, but to"
       intervals [36]:
           xmin = 233.49
           xmax = 239.75
           text = "another person's ear it will sound quite robotic. So raise your pitch on those stressed words."
       intervals [37]:
           xmin = 239.75
           xmax = 247.01
           text = "I want a cup of tea. I want a cup of tea. Now you're probably thinking, great Emma,"
       intervals [38]:
           xmin = 247.01
           xmax = 251.35
           text = "understood that, but which words are stressed and which ones are unstressed? I have a handy"
       intervals [39]:
           xmin = 251.35
           xmax = 256.77
           text = "table for you. What we can do is categorise English words into two different groups. Our"
       intervals [40]:
           xmin = 256.81
           xmax = 264.13
           text = "content words and function words. To name a few content words, nouns, adverbs, adjectives,"
       intervals [41]:
           xmin = 264.13
           xmax = 272.05
           text = "verbs, question words, and negative, auxiliary, modal, and negative words in general. For our"
       intervals [42]:
           xmin = 272.05
           xmax = 279.49
           text = "function words, we have articles, auxiliary verbs, modal verbs, the verb to be, pronouns,"
       intervals [43]:
           xmin = 279.49
           xmax = 286.69
           text = "prepositions, and conjunctions, otherwise known as connectives. So if we go back to our original"
       intervals [44]:
           xmin = 286.69
           xmax = 297.28
           text = "sentence, I want a cup of tea, we have I, it's a pronoun, want, verb, a, article, cup, noun, of,"
       intervals [45]:
           xmin = 297.28
           xmax = 304.78
           text = "preposition, tea, noun. So you can see here how it follows that list that I gave before."
       intervals [46]:
           xmin = 304.78
           xmax = 310.78
           text = "Now let's change our sentence a little bit. I would like a cup of tea. This time we've got"
       intervals [47]:
           xmin = 310.78
           xmax = 315.98
           text = "the pronoun, right, it's the same as before, pronoun, then we've got would, we've got a modal"
       intervals [48]:
           xmin = 315.98
           xmax = 324.42
           text = "verb, but notice I'm not saying I would like. I weaken it. I would like. So would becomes would."
       intervals [49]:
           xmin = 325.06
           xmax = 332.64
           text = "I would like. Like is a verb. I would like. It's the same after that actually, it's the first"
       intervals [50]:
           xmin = 332.64
           xmax = 342.48
           text = "sentence. So we have the article, a, cup, noun, of, preposition, and tea, the noun. Let's take a look"
       intervals [51]:
           xmin = 342.48
           xmax = 349.87
           text = "at this one together. Which of these words do you think will be stressed? Okay, you can pause,"
       intervals [52]:
           xmin = 350.43
           xmax = 361.42
           text = "have a think. She will be late to the party. She will be late to the party. So again, she,"
       intervals [53]:
           xmin = 361.98
           xmax = 369.42
           text = "pronoun, will is our modal verb, and we don't usually stress modal verbs. She will be late."
       intervals [54]:
           xmin = 370.42
           xmax = 379.38
           text = "Late is an adjective, to the, not to the, to the, preposition, article, party, noun."
       intervals [55]:
           xmin = 380.37
           xmax = 386.61
           text = "She will be late to the party. She will be late to the party. Here's another sentence,"
       intervals [56]:
           xmin = 386.61
           xmax = 391.65
           text = "a little bit longer to practise with. Which ones do you think will be stressed here? Feel free to"
       intervals [57]:
           xmin = 391.65
           xmax = 400.42
           text = "pause the video and take your time. Can you go to the shop and buy me some bread? Can you go"
       intervals [58]:
           xmin = 400.42
           xmax = 407.71
           text = "to the shop and buy me some bread? Can, remember, is a modal verb. We're not going to stress it."
       intervals [59]:
           xmin = 408.75
           xmax = 419.17
           text = "Can becomes can. Can you go? Can you go, not to the, to the, to the shop. Can you go to the shop"
       intervals [60]:
           xmin = 419.17
           xmax = 427.09
           text = "and buy me some bread? Some, strong form is some, gets weakened to a schwa. Can you go to the shop"
       intervals [61]:
           xmin = 427.09
           xmax = 432.8
           text = "and buy me some bread? Can you go to the shop and buy me some bread? Now, we sometimes break"
       intervals [62]:
           xmin = 432.8
           xmax = 439.36
           text = "this rule if the word is used for emphasis, contrast, if it's alone in a sentence, or sometimes"
       intervals [63]:
           xmin = 439.36
           xmax = 445.04
           text = "if it comes at the end of a sentence. So let's take this sentence here. Now, in normal sentence"
       intervals [64]:
           xmin = 445.04
           xmax = 453.2
           text = "stress, we could say, I asked her if she wanted fish and chips. I asked her, or asked, it's up to"
       intervals [65]:
           xmin = 453.2
           xmax = 463.84
           text = "you, or asked, asked. I asked her if she wanted fish and chips. Fish and chips. So not fish and"
       intervals [66]:
           xmin = 463.84
           xmax = 470.4
           text = "chips. Fish and chips. Okay? And gets weakened to an. But let's imagine the listener that we're"
       intervals [67]:
           xmin = 470.4
           xmax = 476.0
           text = "speaking to got a bit confused. They thought we said something else, so we need to clarify something."
       intervals [68]:
           xmin = 476.99
           xmax = 487.79
           text = "No, I said fish and chips, not fish or chips. Fish and chips, not fish or chips. Now, I'm giving stress"
       intervals [69]:
           xmin = 487.79
           xmax = 495.47
           text = "to and and or. Otherwise, naturally, it would be said as fish and chips and fisher chips. Fisher"
       intervals [70]:
           xmin = 495.47
           xmax = 501.63
           text = "chips, that sounds really weird, but it's what we would say. Now, for some extra practice and to talk"
       intervals [71]:
           xmin = 501.63
           xmax = 506.11
           text = "about the special surprise that I mentioned at the start of the video, we today are going to be"
       intervals [72]:
           xmin = 506.11
           xmax = 512.59
           text = "using the ELSA app. ELSA is a pronunciation app that focuses on pinpointing your mistakes with"
       intervals [73]:
           xmin = 512.59
           xmax = 518.51
           text = "pronunciation, and it gives you very, very specific corrections. We're going to go through it together."
       intervals [74]:
           xmin = 518.51
           xmax = 523.71
           text = "We're actually going to go through the unit on sentence stress today. Well, we're going to go"
       intervals [75]:
           xmin = 523.71
